hadiths[5]{hadithnumber,text}:
"858","Ibn al-Muqri and Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut for a quarter dinar and above."
"859","Ali ibn Khashram narrated to us, he said: Ismail informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut for a shield worth three dirhams."
"860","Ibn al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, from Rafi ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for palm-pith."
"861","Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam informed us that Ibn Wahb informed them, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), that a man of Muzaynah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think concerning sheep grazing on the mountain? He said: It and its like, and exemplary punishment; there is no cutting in anything of livestock except what the pen shelters, reaching the price of a shield, for in it is cutting of the hand; what does not reach the price of a shield, in it is a fine of twice its value and lashes as exemplary punishment. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think concerning hanging fruit? So he said: It and twice its value with it, and exemplary punishment; and there is no cutting in anything of fruit except what the threshing floor shelters; what is taken from the threshing floor reaching the price of a shield, in it is cutting; and what does not reach the price of a shield, in it is a fine of its like and lashes as exemplary punishment."
"862","Muhammad ibn Yahya, Harun ibn Ishaq, and Abu Zurah al-Razi narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Asbat, from Simak, from Humayd, nephew of Safwan ibn Umayyah, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, he said: I was sleeping in the mosque - and Harun said: sitting in the mosque - upon a cloak worth thirty dirhams. Then a man came and snatched it from me. So the man was seized and brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered him cut. So I came to him and said: Do you cut him for thirty dirhams? I sell it to him and defer its price. He said: Why was that not before you brought him to me?"
